### How do you write and combine predicates?

Define each predicate as a named variable, then compose with .and().

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.Predicate;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class PredicateComposition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
            new Employee("Rajesh", "Loans",   75000.0),
            new Employee("Priya",  "IT",      82000.0),
            new Employee("Amit",   "IT",      45000.0),
            new Employee("Kavita", "IT",      91000.0),
            new Employee("Neha",   "Finance", 78000.0)
        );

        Predicate<Employee> highSalary = e -> e.getSalaryAsDouble() > 50_000;
        Predicate<Employee> inIT = e -> "IT".equals(e.getDepartment());

        List<Employee> result = employees.stream()
            .filter(highSalary.and(inIT))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        result.forEach(e -> System.out.println(
            e.getName() + " (" + e.getDepartment()
                + ", " + e.getSalaryAsDouble() + ")"));
    }
}
```

```text
Priya (IT, 82000.0)
Kavita (IT, 91000.0)
```

Amit is in IT but earns 45000, so he fails the highSalary predicate and is excluded.

### What composition methods does Predicate offer?

| Method | Behaviour | Example |
|---|---|---|
| .and(other) | Both must pass | highSalary.and(inIT) |
| .or(other) | Either must pass | highSalary.or(inIT) |
| .negate() | Inverts result | highSalary.negate() |
| Predicate.not(p) | Static negation (Java 11) | Predicate.not(highSalary) |

```java
// Employees who are NOT in IT
List<Employee> notIT = employees.stream()
    .filter(inIT.negate())
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// Employees who earn high OR are in IT
List<Employee> either = employees.stream()
    .filter(highSalary.or(inIT))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### Why does predicate order matter?

.and() tests the receiver predicate first and short-circuits. Put the cheaper or more selective predicate first.

```java
// If highSalary eliminates 80% of employees,
// putting it first avoids evaluating inIT for those 80%.
filter(highSalary.and(inIT))  // fewer evaluations of inIT

// vs
filter(inIT.and(highSalary))  // evaluates highSalary only for IT employees
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: writing one big boolean expression instead of composable predicates.

```java
// WRONG: not reusable, hard to test individually
.filter(e -> e.getSalaryAsDouble() > 50_000
    && "IT".equals(e.getDepartment()))

// CORRECT: named, reusable, testable
Predicate<Employee> highSalary = e -> e.getSalaryAsDouble() > 50_000;
Predicate<Employee> inIT = e -> "IT".equals(e.getDepartment());
.filter(highSalary.and(inIT))
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Each simple predicate has a clear name, can be unit-tested independently, and can be reused across reports.
- Composition turns filtering rules into named, swappable building blocks, which is far more maintainable than giant boolean expressions.
- .and() returns a new Predicate instance; the original predicates are not modified.
- In a filter chain, multiple .filter() calls are equivalent to a single .filter(a.and(b)), but named composition is more readable.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you negate a predicate?" Use .negate() or Predicate.not() (Java 11+).
- "Can you compose more than two predicates?" Yes: a.and(b).and(c).
- "What is Predicate.isEqual(target)?" A static factory that creates a predicate testing equality to a given target.
